IMPORT prelude

-- Cremation Act 1929 (Western Australia) -- arranging a cremation: who may apply for the permit (ss 2, 8(2)-(3B)), what the
-- medical referee must see before granting it and when the referee must refuse (s 8A, s 12), when the coroner must be
-- involved (s 8(5), s 8A(e), (f), s 14), the deceased's own wishes and the family's objection (s 8A(b), s 13), review of a
-- refusal (s 8(6)), when the cremation may go ahead (ss 3, 9, 10, 14; Form 9's 24 hours), the ashes (s 7), the permit fee
-- (Cremation Regulations 1954 Sch 2 item 3, r 14(2)) and the penalty (s 15).
--
-- SEAT: the family member or executor arranging a cremation. Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it: "Am I
-- an administrator?", "May I apply for the permit?", "Must I make a statutory declaration?", "What will the medical referee
-- do with my application?", "Must the coroner be involved?", "Does the family's objection stop the cremation?", "May I take
-- the ashes away?". The Act addresses the medical referee and the crematorium; what it means for the person arranging is
-- whether a permit must be granted, what must accompany the application and what may be done with the body and the ashes.
-- Where the Act hands a judgment to the medical referee ("is satisfied", "it appears to", "in his opinion"), the coroner,
-- the Attorney General or the Chief Health Officer, that judgment is asked as a fact, never guessed.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 2 ("administrator", "body"), s 3(1), s 7, s 8(2), (3A), (3B), (4A), (5), (6), s 8A(a)-(g), s 9,
-- s 10, s 12, s 13(1), (2), s 14, s 15(1); Cremation Regulations 1954 r 11, r 12, r 14(2), (4), Sch 1 Form 9 (the 24
-- hours), Sch 2 item 3.
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 37196, retrieved 7 September 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_37196.txt; its compilation table runs to the Public Health (Consequential Provisions)
-- Act 2016 (Reprint 6 as at 27 October 2017); s 285(2) of that Act is listed as uncommenced and is not reflected.
-- Regulations: Cremation Regulations 1954, version 04-o0-00, currency from 1 July 2026 (mrdoc 49624, retrieved 20 October
-- 2026), as deposited at ../../../cremation-regulations-1954/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49624.txt; Sch 2 as last
-- amended by SL 2026/112.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: licensing of crematoria and its revocation (ss 4, 5, 6, 16; regs Parts I, II); the appointment of medical
-- referees (s 8(1); r 13); s 3(2) and regs Part V (cremation elsewhere than in a crematorium on religious grounds), asked
-- only as the fact that the cremation is at a licensed crematorium; s 8B (stillborn children); s 8(4B), r 14(1), (3),
-- (5)-(8) (the referee's own housekeeping); the register and notices kept by the crematorium (regs 18-20); s 15(2)-(5);
-- s 17 (regulation-making). The content of the forms beyond what r 12 requires to accompany an application is not encoded.
--
-- BASELINE. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the seat of the person arranging the cremation, with the design
-- defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use
-- as advice.

§ `Cremation Act 1929 (WA) -- arranging a cremation`

§§ `the people and the facts`

-- s 2: "administrator includes executor and any person who, by law or practice, has the best right to apply for
-- administration, and any person having the lawful custody of the body of a deceased person".
-- s 8(3A): "An application for a permit may be made by -- (a) an administrator; or (b) a person acting with the written
-- authority of and on behalf of the administrator; or (c) a person who satisfies a medical referee that no application for a
-- permit is to be made by the administrator ..., and who shows to the medical referee a satisfactory reason why the
-- application is not made by an administrator, and that he is a proper person to make the application."
-- s 13(1): no person shall "apply for ... any permit" if he knows that a spouse, de facto partner or next of kin "has
-- objected in writing". "Best right to apply for administration", "lawful custody" and "next of kin" are borrowed or
-- undefined terms, asked as facts. (Regs r 3 defines "nearest surviving relative" for the regulations only; the Act's "next
-- of kin" is not defined.)
DECLARE Applicant HAS
    `is the executor named in the will` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has the best right to apply for administration` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has lawful custody of the body` IS A BOOLEAN
    `acts with the written authority of and on behalf of the administrator` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the referee is satisfied the administrator will not apply, of my reason, and that I am a proper person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `knows that a spouse, de facto partner or next of kin has objected in writing` IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 8A(g): the doctor who gives the s 8A(a) certificate must not be "(i) the parent child brother or sister of the deceased
-- person or ... the uncle aunt niece or nephew ...; (ii) in partnership with the medical referee; (iii) the medical referee".
-- A spouse, a grandparent or grandchild and a cousin are not in the list.
DECLARE `Doctor's relationship to the deceased` IS ONE OF
    `no relation`
    `parent, child, brother or sister`
    `uncle, aunt, niece or nephew`
    `spouse or de facto partner`
    `another relative`

-- s 12: "No person who knows that, under the terms of any policy of life assurance, will, settlement, or statute or
-- otherwise howsoever, he is entitled, or will become entitled, by reason or in consequence of the death of another person,
-- to any real or personal property, shall give or sign any certificate concerning the death ... for any of the purposes of
-- this Act."
DECLARE Doctor HAS
    `relationship to the deceased` IS A `Doctor's relationship to the deceased`
    `is in partnership with the medical referee` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is the medical referee` IS A BOOLEAN
    `knows they are or will become entitled to property because of the death` IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 14: the Attorney General or any coroner may by written order forbid cremation "either absolutely or until the viscera or
-- any other organs have been removed from the body and lodged or deposited in such place and custody as may be specified".
DECLARE `Order forbidding cremation` IS ONE OF
    `no order`
    `forbidden absolutely`
    `forbidden until organs are removed and lodged`

-- The facts about the death. Fields marked (referee) are the medical referee's judgment, and (coroner) the coroner's,
-- asked as facts.
DECLARE Death HAS
    `died outside Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a certificate of cause of death has been provided` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the certifying doctor` IS A Doctor
    `left a written direction not to be cremated` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Chief Health Officer has ordered the body to be cremated` IS A BOOLEAN
    `by attested will, codicil or signed and attested writing, directed or desired cremation` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a spouse, de facto partner or next of kin has objected in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the fact and cause of death are definitely ascertained (referee)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `appears due to violence or unnatural causes, or suspicious circumstances (referee)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the coroner has signified an intention to investigate (coroner)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a coroner has certified that the permit may be granted (coroner)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `order under section 14` IS AN `Order forbidding cremation`
    `the organs have been removed and lodged as the order specifies` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `2, 8(3A), 8(3B) -- may I apply, and must I declare`

@export Am I an administrator of the deceased, under the Cremation Act 1929 (WA) s 2?
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant @desc Who you are in relation to the deceased and the body
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an administrator` `me` IF
        `me`'s `is the executor named in the will`
    OR  `me`'s `has the best right to apply for administration`
    OR  `me`'s `has lawful custody of the body`

-- s 8(3A): who may make an application.
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am within section 8(3A)` `me` IF
        `Am I an administrator` `me`
    OR  `me`'s `acts with the written authority of and on behalf of the administrator`
    OR  `me`'s `the referee is satisfied the administrator will not apply, of my reason, and that I am a proper person`

-- s 13(1): the objection binds unless "the deceased shall, by his will or any codicil or any memorandum or writing signed
-- by him and attested, have directed or expressed a desire that his body is to be cremated".
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the deceased's attested wish overrides objections` `the death` IF
    `the death`'s `by attested will, codicil or signed and attested writing, directed or desired cremation`

@export May I apply for a permit to cremate the deceased (Cremation Act 1929 (WA) ss 8(3A), 13(1))?
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant @desc Who you are, and what you know of any objection
      `the death` IS A Death @desc The deceased's written wishes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I apply for the permit` `me` `the death` IF
        `I am within section 8(3A)` `me`
    AND (   (NOT `me`'s `knows that a spouse, de facto partner or next of kin has objected in writing`)
         OR `the deceased's attested wish overrides objections` `the death`)

-- s 8(3B): "Where the application for a permit is not made by an administrator, the person making the application shall --
-- (a) make a statutory declaration in the prescribed form setting out the grounds upon which he claims authority to make the
-- application; and (b) supply such further information ... as the medical referee may require." Form 6's declaration is
-- marked "(This section not to be completed by administrator)".
@export Must I make a statutory declaration with my application for a cremation permit (s 8(3B))?
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant @desc Who you are in relation to the deceased and the body
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I make a statutory declaration` `me` IF
    NOT `Am I an administrator` `me`

§§ `8(4A), Regulations Sch 2 item 3, r 14(2) -- the fee`

-- s 8(4A): "A person who applies for a permit shall pay the prescribed fee to the medical referee issuing the permit."
-- Sch 2 item 3: "(a) given between 9 am and 5 pm Monday to Friday, or between 9 am and noon Saturday, excluding public
-- holidays $89.10; (b) given at any other time $147.40". The fee turns on when the permit is GIVEN. "Between 9 am and 5 pm"
-- is read as from 9:00 up to but not including 17:00 (an inference); the hour is on a 24-hour clock.
DECLARE `Day the permit is given` IS ONE OF
    `Monday to Friday`
    `Saturday`
    `Sunday`
    `public holiday`

`the ordinary-hours permit fee` MEANS 89.10
`the out-of-hours permit fee` MEANS 147.40

GIVEN `the day` IS A `Day the permit is given`
      `the hour` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the permit is given in ordinary hours` `the day` `the hour` IF
        (`the day` EQUALS `Monday to Friday` AND `the hour` AT LEAST 9 AND `the hour` LESS THAN 17)
    OR  (`the day` EQUALS `Saturday` AND `the hour` AT LEAST 9 AND `the hour` LESS THAN 12)

@export What fee must I pay the medical referee for a cremation permit, in dollars (s 8(4A); Regulations Sch 2 item 3)?
GIVEN `the day` IS A `Day the permit is given` @desc The day the permit is given
      `the hour` IS A NUMBER @desc The time it is given, as an hour on a 24-hour clock (14.5 is 2:30 pm)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What fee must I pay for the permit` `the day` `the hour` IS
    IF `the permit is given in ordinary hours` `the day` `the hour`
    THEN `the ordinary-hours permit fee`
    ELSE `the out-of-hours permit fee`

§§ `8A(a), (aa), (g), 12; Regulations r 11, 12 -- the certificates`

-- s 8A(a): no permit "unless a certificate of cause of death has been provided under section 44 of the Births, Deaths and
-- Marriages Registration Act 1998"; (aa) for a death outside the State, a certificate "under the law of the place". Neither
-- paragraph has an exception for a death reported to a coroner.
-- r 12: every application (on Form 6, r 11) "shall be accompanied by the fee prescribed in Schedule 2 item 3 and a
-- certificate in accordance with -- (a) Schedule 1 Form 7, completed by a medical practitioner; or (b) Schedule 1 Form 8,
-- completed by the Coroner; or (c) the Coroners Regulations 1997 Schedule 1 Form 4, completed by the Coroner."
-- s 10: "No medical referee shall issue any permit without having first received the proper certificate or certificates".
-- s 12 is read as making a certificate given by an interested doctor not a "proper certificate" (an inference: s 12 is a
-- prohibition on the doctor, and s 10 the referee's duty).
@export Can the certifying doctor's certificate of cause of death support a cremation permit (s 8A(g), s 12)?
GIVEN `the doctor` IS A Doctor @desc The doctor who certified the cause of death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the doctor's certificate support the permit` `the doctor` IF
        (NOT (`the doctor`'s `relationship to the deceased` EQUALS `parent, child, brother or sister`))
    AND (NOT (`the doctor`'s `relationship to the deceased` EQUALS `uncle, aunt, niece or nephew`))
    AND (NOT `the doctor`'s `is in partnership with the medical referee`)
    AND (NOT `the doctor`'s `is the medical referee`)
    AND (NOT `the doctor`'s `knows they are or will become entitled to property because of the death`)

GIVEN `the death` IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the certificate requirement is met` `the death` IF
        `the death`'s `a certificate of cause of death has been provided`
    AND `Can the doctor's certificate support the permit` (`the death`'s `the certifying doctor`)

§§ `8A(b), 13 -- the deceased's own wishes and the family's objection`

-- s 8A(b): no permit "where the deceased person has left a written direction that his body is not to be cremated, except
-- where the Chief Health Officer orders the body to be cremated" under the Health (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1911 or the
-- Public Health Act 2016. A written direction is enough; it need not be signed or attested.
@export Does the deceased's own written direction against cremation stop the cremation (s 8A(b))?
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death @desc The deceased's written wishes and any order of the Chief Health Officer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the deceased's direction stop the cremation` `the death` IF
        `the death`'s `left a written direction not to be cremated`
    AND (NOT `the death`'s `the Chief Health Officer has ordered the body to be cremated`)

-- s 13(1): "no person shall cremate, or apply for or grant any permit for the cremation of, the body ... if he knows that
-- a person who was married to, or in a de facto relationship with, the deceased immediately before the death ..., or any
-- person who is next of kin of the deceased has objected in writing to the body being cremated, unless the deceased shall,
-- by his will or any codicil or any memorandum or writing signed by him and attested, have directed or expressed a desire
-- that his body is to be cremated." Form 6 asks the applicant "Do you know of anyone who objects ...?", so the objection
-- is taken to be known to the referee (an inference).
@export Does a written objection by the spouse, de facto partner or next of kin stop the cremation (s 13(1))?
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death @desc The objection, and the deceased's attested wishes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the family's objection stop the cremation` `the death` IF
        `the death`'s `a spouse, de facto partner or next of kin has objected in writing`
    AND (NOT `the deceased's attested wish overrides objections` `the death`)

-- s 13(2): "It shall be the duty of the administrator of a deceased person to use all reasonable endeavours to have the
-- direction or desire contained or expressed in such will, codicil, memorandum, or writing carried into effect."
@export Must I use all reasonable endeavours to have the deceased cremated as they directed (s 13(2))?
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant @desc Who you are in relation to the deceased and the body
      `the death` IS A Death @desc The deceased's attested wishes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I try to carry out the deceased's wish to be cremated` `me` `the death` IF
        `Am I an administrator` `me`
    AND `the deceased's attested wish overrides objections` `the death`

§§ `8(2), 8(5), 8A -- what the medical referee will do`

-- s 8(5): where it appears to the referee that the death "is due to violence or unnatural causes, or if there are in his
-- opinion suspicious circumstances", the referee "shall refuse the application and report the refusal and the reason for
-- the refusal to the coroner residing nearest to the place where the body is lying". s 8A(e): after such a refusal no
-- permit "unless and until he receives from a coroner a certificate in the prescribed form authorising him to grant the
-- permit". s 8A(f): likewise "where the coroner has signified his intention to investigate the death".
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the referee must refuse and report to the coroner` `the death` IF
        `the death`'s `appears due to violence or unnatural causes, or suspicious circumstances (referee)`
    AND (NOT `the death`'s `a coroner has certified that the permit may be granted (coroner)`)

GIVEN `the death` IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the coroner's certificate is awaited` `the death` IF
        `the death`'s `the coroner has signified an intention to investigate (coroner)`
    AND (NOT `the death`'s `a coroner has certified that the permit may be granted (coroner)`)

-- The other bars: s 8A(a)/(aa) and (g) with s 12; s 8A(b); s 8A(c) (the referee "is satisfied that the applicant ... is
-- entitled or authorised to make the application"); s 8A(d) ("the fact and cause of death ... are definitely
-- ascertained"); s 13(1); and r 11, 12 (Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate).
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant
      `the death` IS A Death
      `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `another bar applies` `me` `the death` `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate` IF
        (NOT `the certificate requirement is met` `the death`)
    OR  `Does the deceased's direction stop the cremation` `the death`
    OR  (NOT `I am within section 8(3A)` `me`)
    OR  (NOT `the death`'s `the fact and cause of death are definitely ascertained (referee)`)
    OR  `Does the family's objection stop the cremation` `the death`
    OR  (NOT `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate`)

-- s 8(2): if the referee "is satisfied that the applicant is an administrator or other duly authorised person to hold the
-- permit, he SHALL subject to the provisions of this Act, grant the permit." Granting is a duty, not a discretion.
DECLARE `What the referee will do` IS ONE OF
    `grant the permit, as s 8(2) requires`
    `refuse it and report the death to the coroner (s 8(5)); a permit only on a coroner's certificate`
    `refuse it until the coroner certifies that it may be granted (s 8A(f))`
    `refuse it; I may ask the State Administrative Tribunal to review the refusal (s 8(6))`

@export What will the medical referee do with my application for a cremation permit (Cremation Act 1929 (WA) ss 8, 8A, 13)?
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant @desc Who you are, and what you know of any objection
      `the death` IS A Death @desc The death, the certificates, the deceased's wishes and any objection or coronial involvement
      `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application is on Form 6, with the fee and a doctor's Form 7 certificate or a coroner's certificate
GIVETH A `What the referee will do`
DECIDE `What will the referee do with my application` `me` `the death` `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `the referee must refuse and report to the coroner` `the death`
        THEN `refuse it and report the death to the coroner (s 8(5)); a permit only on a coroner's certificate`
      IF `the coroner's certificate is awaited` `the death`
        THEN `refuse it until the coroner certifies that it may be granted (s 8A(f))`
      IF `another bar applies` `me` `the death` `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate`
        THEN `refuse it; I may ask the State Administrative Tribunal to review the refusal (s 8(6))`
      OTHERWISE `grant the permit, as s 8(2) requires`

GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant
      `the death` IS A Death
      `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will I be granted the permit` `me` `the death` `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate` IF
    `What will the referee do with my application` `me` `the death` `the application is on Form 6 with the fee and a Form 7 or coroner's certificate`
        EQUALS `grant the permit, as s 8(2) requires`

-- When the coroner is involved: s 8(5) (the referee must report), s 8A(f) (the coroner is investigating) and s 14 (an order
-- forbidding cremation, absolutely or until organs are removed).
@export Must the coroner be involved before the cremation can go ahead (s 8(5), s 8A(e), (f), s 14)?
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death @desc The death, as it appears to the referee, and anything the coroner has done
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the coroner be involved` `the death` IF
        `the referee must refuse and report to the coroner` `the death`
    OR  `the coroner's certificate is awaited` `the death`
    OR  (NOT (`the death`'s `order under section 14` EQUALS `no order`))

-- s 8(6): "Where, except as provided in the last preceding subsection, a medical referee refuses to issue a permit, the
-- applicant may apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review". r 14(4): the referee must tell the applicant of
-- that right, but "need not advise the applicant of his reasons". A refusal under s 8(5) is not reviewable.
@export If the medical referee refuses my permit, may I ask the State Administrative Tribunal to review it (s 8(6))?
GIVEN `the refusal was under section 8(5)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the referee refused because the death appeared violent, unnatural or suspicious
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I ask the Tribunal to review the refusal` `the refusal was under section 8(5)` IF
    NOT `the refusal was under section 8(5)`

§§ `3, 9, 10, 13, 14; Form 9 -- may the cremation go ahead`

-- s 3(1): no cremation "elsewhere than in a licensed crematorium". s 9: a permit lawfully granted is "sufficient authority"
-- for cremation by the licensees at their crematorium. s 10: "no person shall cremate or permit the cremation of any human
-- body without first having received the necessary permit". s 13(1) also bars cremating where an objection is known. s 14:
-- an order forbidding cremation binds "every person to whose knowledge it shall come". Form 9 (the permit, r 14(5)): "This
-- permit shall not be valid until 24 hours have elapsed from the time of death".
`hours after death before a permit is valid` MEANS 24

GIVEN `the death` IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 14 order forbids cremation now` `the death` IF
    CONSIDER `the death`'s `order under section 14`
    WHEN `no order` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `forbidden absolutely` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `forbidden until organs are removed and lodged` THEN
        NOT `the death`'s `the organs have been removed and lodged as the order specifies`

@export May the cremation go ahead now (Cremation Act 1929 (WA) ss 3, 9, 10, 13, 14; Form 9)?
GIVEN `the death` IS A Death @desc The death, any objection and any order forbidding cremation
      `a permit has been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical referee has granted the permit
      `hours since death` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since the death
      `at a licensed crematorium` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cremation is to be at a crematorium licensed under the Act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the cremation go ahead now` `the death` `a permit has been granted` `hours since death` `at a licensed crematorium` IF
        `a permit has been granted`
    AND `hours since death` AT LEAST `hours after death before a permit is valid`
    AND `at a licensed crematorium`
    AND (NOT `a section 14 order forbids cremation now` `the death`)
    AND (NOT `Does the family's objection stop the cremation` `the death`)

§§ `7 -- the ashes`

-- s 7(1): where "the person who obtained the permit ... desires to dispose of the ashes ... otherwise than by burial upon
-- the site of the crematorium, it shall be lawful for the [cemetery Board] ... or the association in whose crematorium the
-- body was cremated, to deliver the said ashes to the said person for removal from the crematorium." (2): otherwise the
-- ashes "shall not be removed from the crematorium ... except for the purpose of burial in the site of the crematorium."
-- The Act makes delivery LAWFUL; it does not oblige the crematorium to deliver, and it says nothing of the ashes once
-- removed.
@export May the ashes be delivered to me to take away from the crematorium (s 7)?
GIVEN `I obtained the permit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who obtained the cremation permit
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the ashes be delivered to me` `I obtained the permit` IF
    `I obtained the permit`

@export May the ashes be removed from the crematorium for this purpose (s 7(2))?
GIVEN `to the person who obtained the permit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ashes go to the person who obtained the permit
      `for burial on the crematorium site` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they are being moved only to be buried on the crematorium's own site
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the ashes be removed` `to the person who obtained the permit` `for burial on the crematorium site` IF
    `to the person who obtained the permit` OR `for burial on the crematorium site`

§§ `13, 15 -- offences`

-- s 15(1): "Any person contravening by act or omission any provision of this Act shall be deemed to have committed an
-- offence, and shall be liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding $400 or to imprisonment for not exceeding one
-- year." (2) an attempt is punishable as if committed; (3) so is being "party or privy".
`maximum fine in dollars` MEANS 400
`maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS 12

@export Would I commit an offence by applying for the permit (s 13(1), s 15)?
GIVEN `me` IS AN Applicant @desc Who you are, and what you know of any objection
      `the death` IS A Death @desc The deceased's attested wishes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Would applying be an offence` `me` `the death` IF
        `me`'s `knows that a spouse, de facto partner or next of kin has objected in writing`
    AND (NOT `the deceased's attested wish overrides objections` `the death`)

@export What is the most I can be fined for an offence against the Cremation Act 1929 (WA), in dollars (s 15(1))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined` IS `maximum fine in dollars`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for an offence against the Cremation Act 1929 (WA), in months (s 15(1))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest I can be imprisoned in months` IS `maximum imprisonment in months`
